WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:04.318
In your second month of job hunting, you have sent forty applications without a single reply.

2
00:00:04.818 --> 00:00:11.487
The person who finally helps is a college roommate you have not spoken to for three years: ‘Our team is hiring.’

3
00:00:12.287 --> 00:00:15.659
The close friend you eat with every day could not help. Why?

4
00:00:16.559 --> 00:00:20.609
Sociologist Mark Granovetter called this the strength of weak ties.

5
00:00:21.509 --> 00:00:26.208
He interviewed a group of people in professional, technical and managerial jobs who had recently changed jobs in a Boston suburb.

6
00:00:26.608 --> 00:00:32.009
Among the 54 who found work through a contact, fewer than one in five saw that contact frequently.

7
00:00:32.359 --> 00:00:36.930
Most saw them occasionally, and nearly three in ten saw them at most once a year.

8
00:00:37.730 --> 00:00:44.015
His explanation was that close friends tend to know similar people and hear similar information.

9
00:00:44.415 --> 00:00:50.767
A weaker connection belongs to another circle and can act like a bridge, bringing information from outside yours.

10
00:00:51.567 --> 00:00:57.680
Of course, this is a small sample from half a century ago, showing an interview association, not an experiment.

11
00:00:58.080 --> 00:01:05.346
The author himself raised an objection: people have more acquaintances to begin with, so what does this proportion establish? He left that unsettled here.

12
00:01:06.046 --> 00:01:13.954
So do not read this as saying acquaintances are more useful than friends. Close friends may be more willing to help; weaker ties may know something you do not.

13
00:01:14.854 --> 00:01:23.531
Who brought you the last piece of news that really changed your plans—a close friend, or someone you had not contacted in a long time?

14
00:01:24.531 --> 00:01:26.366
You can say hello, even after a long time.
